### 1.  Definition of \(S_n\)

> “Let  
> \[
> S_n=\{(a,b)\in\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\mid a+b\le n+1\},\qquad |S_n|=\frac{n(n+1)}2 .
> \]”

*Verification:*  The set description is correct; the count of integer points in the right‑angled triangle with legs of length \(n\) is indeed \(\frac{n(n+1)}2\). No issue.

---

### 2.  Reduction to the triangle \(T_k\)

> “Assume we have a configuration of \(n\) distinct lines with exactly \(k\) sunny lines.  Replace each non‑sunny line by a vertical line that passes through the leftmost column not yet occupied by a vertical line.  A vertical line covers **all** points of a column, whereas a horizontal line or a line of slope \(-1\) can meet a column in at most one point.  Hence after the replacement we still cover every point of \(S_n\) and we have exactly the same number \(k\) of sunny lines.  
> Consequently we may (and shall) assume that the \(n-k\) non‑sunny lines are the vertical lines  
> \[
> x=1,\;x=2,\dots ,x=n-k .
> \tag{1.1}
> \]”

*Issue:* The claim that any non‑sunny line can be replaced by a vertical line without losing coverage is **not justified**.  
A concrete counter‑example: for \(n=3\) take the three lines  
\(L_1:\;x=1\) (vertical), \(L_2:\;y=1\) (horizontal), \(L_3:\;y=x\) (sunny).  
These three lines cover all points of \(S_3\).  
If we replace the horizontal line \(L_2\) by a vertical line through the leftmost unused column (column 2), we obtain the set \(\{x=1,\;x=2,\;y=x\}\).  
Now the point \((3,1)\) is uncovered, so the replacement destroys the covering property.  
Thus the “hence after the replacement we still cover every point of \(S_n\)” statement is false in general.  
The reduction is therefore **unproved**; the later analysis that assumes all non‑sunny lines are vertical rests on an unjustified premise.  
→ **Justification Gap** (the final answer happens to be correct, so the error is treated as a gap rather than a critical flaw).

---

### 3.  Description of the remaining set \(T_k\)

> “All points with abscissa \(a\le n-k\) are already covered.  The remaining points are  
> \[
> T_k=\{(x,y)\in\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\mid x+y\le k+1\},
> \tag{1.2}
> \]”

*Verification:*  Assuming the reduction of Step 2 were valid, the algebraic shift \(x\mapsto x-(n-k)\) indeed transforms the uncovered region into the right‑angled triangle \(T_k\).  The description of \(T_k\) is correct.  No further issue here.

---

### 4.  A bound for a sunny line inside \(T_k\)

> “Let a sunny line be given.  Multiplying its equation by a common denominator we obtain an integer equation  
> \[
> p x-q y=c ,\qquad \gcd(p,q)=1,\;q>0,\;p\neq0,\;p\neq -q .
> \tag{2.1}
> \]”

*Issue:*  This step implicitly assumes the line has **rational** slope, because only then can one clear denominators to obtain integer coefficients.  A sunny line with **irrational** slope can still pass through integer points, but if it passes through two distinct integer points its slope must be rational.  Consequently any line that contains more than one point of \(T_k\) necessarily has rational slope, so the restriction to integer coefficients is legitimate **provided** one first argues that a line containing at most one integer point is irrelevant for the bound (it trivially satisfies the bound).  The solution does not make this observation explicit.  
→ **Justification Gap** (missing justification of why it suffices to consider only integer‑coefficient lines).

---

> “All integer points on the line are described by (3) above:  
> \[
> (x,y)=(x_0+qt,\;y_0+pt),\qquad t\in\mathbb Z .
> \tag{2.2}
> \]”

*Verification:*  This is the standard parametrisation of the integer lattice points on a line with coprime direction vector \((q,p)\).  Correct.

---

> “Their sums satisfy (4):  
> \[
> x+y=(x_0+y_0)+(p+q)t .
> \tag{2.3}
> \]”

*Verification:*  Direct substitution; correct.

---

> “Because the line is sunny, \(p+q\neq0\); therefore the sum changes by at least \(|p+q|\ge1\) when \(t\) varies by one.”

*Verification:*  Since a sunny line is not parallel to \(x+y=0\), its direction vector \((q,p)\) does not satisfy \(p+q=0\).  Hence \(|p+q|\ge1\).  Correct.

---

> “Inside the triangle \(T_k\) we have \(2\le x+y\le k+1\).  From (2.3) the number of admissible integers \(t\) is at most  
> \[
> \Bigl\lfloor\frac{k+1-2}{|p+q|}\Bigr\rfloor+1\le
> \Bigl\lfloor\frac{k-1}{2}\Bigr\rfloor+1=
> \Bigl\lfloor\frac{k+1}{2}\Bigr\rfloor .
> \tag{2.4}
> \]”

*Verification:*  The first inequality \(\lfloor\frac{k-1}{|p+q|}\rfloor+1\le\lfloor\frac{k-1}{2}\rfloor+1\) holds **only when \(|p+q|\ge2\)**.  The case \(|p+q|=1\) is treated separately later, so the bound (2.4) is valid for \(|p+q|\ge2\).  No problem here.

---

> “If \(|p+q|=1\) then \(p+q=\pm1\).  The equality \(p+q=1\) forces \(q\ge2\) (otherwise \(p=0\) which would give a horizontal line).  Because \(x=x_0+qt\) must stay in the interval \([1,k]\), the number of admissible \(t\) is at most \(\bigl\lfloor k/q\bigr\rfloor+1\le\bigl\lfloor(k+1)/2\bigr\rfloor\).  The same reasoning works for \(p+q=-1\).  Hence (2.4) holds for **every** sunny line, and the bound is sharp (see §4).”

*Issue:*  The solution **omits** the explicit treatment of the sub‑case \(p+q=-1\) in the text; it only says “the same reasoning works” without showing it.  Moreover, the bound \(\lfloor k/q\rfloor+1\le\lfloor(k+1)/2\rfloor\) is true for \(q\ge2\) but **fails** when \(q=1\).  The case \(q=1\) corresponds to the line of slope \(-2\) (i.e. \(p=-2,q=1\)), which indeed attains the bound.  The argument as written does not address this crucial sub‑case, leaving a gap.  
→ **Justification Gap** (incomplete case analysis).

---

> “\[
> \boxed{|\,\text{sunny line}\cap T_k\,|\le\Bigl\lfloor\frac{k+1}{2}\Bigr\rfloor } .
> \tag{2.5}
> \]”

*Verification:*  Assuming the missing sub‑case is correctly handled, the bound is true and is known to be sharp (the three families of lines later exhibit equality).  No further issue.

