oor=m+1\\)\n> and (3.2) becomes an equality:\n> \\[\n> (2m+1)(m+1)=\\frac{(2m+1)(2m+2)}2 .\n> \\tag{3.3}\n> \\]  \n> Consequently every sunny line must contain **exactly** \\(m+1\\) points of\n> \\(T_{2m+1}\\), and the \\(k\\) lines must be pairwise disjoint.\u201d\n\n*Verification:*  The arithmetic is correct; equality forces each line to achieve the maximal possible number of points, and the point\u2011sets must be disjoint.  No issue.\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Sunny lines that attain the bound\n\n> \u201cEquation (2.1) with \\(|p+q|=2\\) gives three possibilities:\n> * \\(p=q=1\\)\u2003(slope \\(1\\));\n> * \\(p=-2,\\;q=1\\)\u2003(slope \\(-2\\));\n> * \\(p=-1,\\;q=2\\)\u2003(slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)).\u201d\n\n*Issue:*  The pairs \\((p,q)=(-2,1)\\) and \\((-1,2)\\) satisfy \\(|p+q|=1\\), **not** \\(|p+q|=2\\).  The statement is factually incorrect; it is a typographical/presentation error.  The three lines listed are indeed the three families that attain the bound, but the justification for why they arise from \\(|p+q|=2\\) is wrong.  \n\u2192 **Justification Gap** (presentation error).\n\n---\n\n> \u201cFor \\(k=2m+1\\) the corresponding lines are exactly the three lines listed in (10).  A direct substitution shows that each of them contains \\(m+1\\) points of \\(T_{2m+1}\\) and that the three point\u2011sets are disjoint.  No other sunny line can contain \\(m+1\\) points, because any other choice would have \\(|p+q|\\ge3\\) or \\(|p+q|=1\\) with \\(q\\ge2\\), and then (2.4) gives a strictly smaller upper bound.\u201d\n\n*Verification:*  The claim that the three families (slope\u202f1,\u202f\u22122,\u202f\u2212\u00bd) each contain exactly \\(m+1\\) points of \\(T_{2m+1}\\) is correct; a short computation (as in the solution) confirms it.  The argument that any other line must have \\(|p+q|\\ge3\\) or \\(|p+q|=1\\) with \\(q\\ge2\\) and therefore cannot reach the bound is sound, provided the missing case \\(p+q=-1, q=1\\) (the slope\u202f\u22122 line) is included \u2013 which it is, but the earlier mis\u2011statement obscures this.  Overall the conclusion that only these three families can achieve the bound is valid.  No further issue.\n\n---\n\n> \u201cThus, to cover \\(T_{2m+1}\\) with \\(2m+1\\) sunny lines we must use **only**\n> the three lines (10).  Hence we need \\(2m+1\\le3\\); i.e. \\(k=1\\) or\n> \\(k=3\\).\u201d\n\n*Verification:*  Since the three families are the only ones that can achieve the maximal point\u2011count, and we need \\(2m+1\\) distinct lines, we can use at most three distinct families; therefore \\(2m+1\\le3\\).  The deduction is correct.\n\n---\n\n### 7.  Constructions for the admissible values\n\n#### (a)  \\(k=0\\)\n\n> \u201cThe \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) are all non\u2011sunny and cover\n> every point of \\(S_n\\).\u201d\n\n*Verification:*  Each column \\(x=a\\) (with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\)) is covered, and all points of \\(S_n\\) lie in those columns.  Correct.\n\n#### (b)  \\(k=1\\)\n\n> \u201cUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).  \n> The only uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\).  The sunny line  \n> \\[\n> \\ell:\\; y=2x-2n+1\n> \\]  \n> passes through \\((n,1)\\) and is not parallel to any forbidden direction.\n> Together the \\(n\\) lines satisfy the requirements.\u201d\n\n*Verification:*  For any \\(n\\ge3\\), the vertical lines cover all points with \\(x\\le n-1\\).  The point \\((n,1)\\) is indeed the only remaining point because any point with \\(x=n\\) must have \\(y\\le1\\) (since \\(n+y\\le n+1\\)).  Substituting \\(x=n\\) into \\(\\ell\\) gives \\(y=2n-2n+1=1\\).  The slope \\(2\\) is not \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  Hence the construction works for every \\(n\\).  No issue.\n\n#### (c)  \\(k=3\\)\n\n> \u201cUse the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\).  \n> The uncovered set is \\(T_3\\).  The three sunny lines  \n> \\[\n> \\begin{aligned}\n> L_{1}&: y=x,\\\\\n> L_{2}&: y=-2x+5,\\\\\n> L_{3}&: y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\n> \\end{aligned}\n> \\]  \n> cont